; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED -- there is no upstream .bddl for this task.
;
; provenance.tier: designed
; source-of-record: robosimstudio/tasks/film_peel/suite.py (deleted by this port)
; re-staged 2026-09-10 onto the clip's phone (sample_videos/film peeling.mp4)
;
; THE HALF THAT IS NOT IN THE VIDEO. A clean peel and one that tears the film
; are the SAME FOOTAGE. They differ only in a tension the episode passed
; through, and a measure read at the final step cannot see it -- which is the
; whole reason this suite exists and the whole reason `peak` exists.
;
; This file is `panel.task` with ONE NUMBER ADDED: the 0.30 N tension budget
; below. Everything else -- the scene, the sheet, the peel gate, the reward
; measure, the artifacts -- is identical, deliberately, so that a diff of the
; two documents is a diff of what the budget does and of nothing else. Where
; `panel` merely RECORDS the load, this one SCORES on it, in two places that
; have to agree: the goal's second clause and the reward's mask.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem PEEL_THE_FILM_OFF_THE_PHONE_GENTLY)


  (:language peel the protective film off the phone without tearing it)

  ; The cell, the control mode and the episode count are rgbs.suite's; this is
  ; the delta.
  (:staging
    ; The suite's own pairing, restated because this document used to override
    ; it: the RIGHT hand peels and the LEFT hand holds the phone. That is the
    ; clip -- the left YAM pinches the phone's bottom-left corner across its
    ; thickness and holds it while the right YAM takes the film at the top edge
    ; -- and it is also the reach: the tab stands at the phone's raised top
    ; edge on the centreline, and the right arm solves the pinch, the whole
    ; peel arc back down the face and the carry to the mat on its own side to
    ; 0.0 mm (routine_peel.py records the band). (:arms-solid true) matters
    ; more here than it did with one arm working: both hands are over one
    ; 147 mm phone and the peel arc passes over the holding hand.
    (:embodiment yam_i2rt (:work-arm right) (:support-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
    ; A named arrangement, never a solver config. It is the tape suite's
    ; cloth_vbd@ref with one number changed, and every floor kept: 60 Hz over
    ; 20 substeps with 80 VBD iterations is a stability FLOOR rather than a
    ; choice -- at a coarser dt the film tunnels through the substrate, and a
    ; drag on top of that escalates to NaN, and a thinner film than the tape's
    ; makes that worse. The one change is a 2 mm rigid contact gap where the
    ; reference leaves Newton's 0.1 m: two hands of 50-piece fingertips over
    ; one 147 mm phone store thousands of within-ten-centimetres pairs, more
    ; than MuJoCo's 2048 slots, and the overflow kills the process mid-peel
    ; (measured; peel/layout.py records the counts). `handle` keeps the
    ; reference arrangement: one hand on one box never gets near the ceiling.
    (:physics cloth_vbd_bimanual@v1)
    ; The suite rig, aimed at the centre of the phone's glass in the face's own
    ; plane -- where the film is bonded and where the peel front travels. Its
    ; two clip views (photo, inset) frame the phone the way the reference does.
    (:cameras peel.layout:PANEL_VIEWS)
    ; Two arms: one peels and one holds the phone.
    (:arms 2)
    ; The routine's take is about 1200 control steps at its 0.05 m/s peel:
    ; ~200 for the holding hand to arrive and stall on the corner, ~150 for
    ; the pinch and latch, ~500 for the arc, ~300 for the carry, the drop and
    ; both retreats. 1800 leaves room for the arc to run its full 0.36 m.
    (:max-steps 1800)
  )

  ; Typed by the PLACED records: `film_phone` is the film's declaration with
  ; the factory that lays it up the phone's glass, tab running on past the
  ; raised top edge. Where things are is the asset's own arithmetic
  ; (peel/layout.py, peel/phone.py); a document names the thing.
  (:objects
    film - film_phone
  )

  (:fixtures
    ; The phone the film is stuck to, welded leaning back 30 degrees at the
    ; centreline with its bottom edge toward the arms, and the printed stand it
    ; leans in. Both are scenery with collision: the holding hand closes on the
    ; phone's corner, and the stand is what the pad under that corner must not
    ; touch -- which is why it is narrower than the clip's (phone.py).
    phone - phone
    cradle - phone_cradle
  )

  ; No regions. Nothing here is scored on being inside a box.

  ; The film starts stuck down, and its tab starts held: a drooping flap and a
  ; presented one are different starting worlds. Both are constraints to be
  ; verified after settling, not a layout -- where the sheet is laid is the
  ; loader's business.
  (:init
    (adhered film)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; TWO CLAUSES, and the second is the whole suite.
  ;
  ; (once (>= (broken-fraction film) 0.95))
  ;     Peeled. STICKY, because a broken bond never re-forms -- a peel is not
  ;     something the world can undo, and a non-sticky reading would let a
  ;     successful peel un-succeed because the film fell over. 0.95 rather than
  ;     1.0: a handful of bonds under the far edge can sit inside their break
  ;     threshold indefinitely while the film is plainly off, and waiting for
  ;     them measures patience.
  ;
  ; (<= (peak (peel-excess film)) 0.30)
  ;     Gently. `peel-excess` is the intact bonds' total restoring force NET OF
  ;     THE STANDING LOAD the field carries before anything touches it (a
  ;     placement artefact -- see rgbs/tasks/rgbs/peel/film.py), so the
  ;     budget is stated against a peel rather than against a build convention.
  ;
  ;     NOT LATCHED, and it must not be. The peel latches; a budget can be blown
  ;     AFTER the peel completes, so latching the peel and then and-ing a budget
  ;     would let a policy finish gently and then wrench. Evaluated fresh every
  ;     step -- coherent because `peak` is monotone, so once the budget is blown
  ;     success can never come back.
  ;
  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; WHERE 0.30 N COMES FROM. Transcribed verbatim from the Python's budget_note,
  ; because a threshold whose justification survives only in a deleted file is a
  ; threshold nothing can check:
  ;
  ;   gated on peel GEOMETRY, not speed. Speed does not separate a gentle peel
  ;   from a violent one because the cohesive-zone model is rate-independent
  ;   (force is k times opening, and a bond breaks on displacement) -- measured
  ;   on the original 60 mm film as 0.0490 N at the shipped lift speed against
  ;   0.0447 N at 5.5x. Pulling at the wrong ANGLE does separate them. Measured
  ;   on the flat-panel scene this document used to stage, with the measured
  ;   hand (grip point at the pads, not the MJCF site), the full-pose pinch
  ;   (jaws across the film, roll solved, both pi-twins tried against the wrist
  ;   limit) and the whole handle column latched -- a clamp bar, not a point
  ;   grip: the shipped peel peaked at 0.1601 N; a top-down grasp (lift waypoint
  ;   solving 94.9 mm off) peaked at 0.7521 N at the same ~0.95 broken fraction.
  ;   0.30 N sat 1.9x above the clean peel -- headroom for a policy less exact
  ;   than the script -- and 2.5x below the mis-angled one.
  ;
  ; THAT NUMBER WAS MEASURED ON THE FLAT PANEL, AND THIS IS THE PHONE. The
  ; sheet is now 140 x 60 mm on a face leaning back 30 degrees, folded back on
  ; itself by a routine rather than hauled in a line by the graph, and the
  ; budget has NOT been re-derived for it: it stands as the number it was, and
  ; what the routine's peel peaks at against it is recorded by the run
  ; (`peak_excess` in its row extras), not adjusted here. Re-measure before
  ; tuning it, in either direction.
  (:goal
    (And
      (once (>= (broken-fraction film) 0.95))
      (<= (peak (peel-excess film)) 0.30)
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Progress toward the peel gate, masked to zero by the SAME budget the goal
  ; gates on.
  ;
  ; Zeroed rather than reduced, and that is the point of the mask: a film peeled
  ; 80% of the way and then torn is not 0.8 of a success, and a reward that
  ; merely dipped would let a policy trade tearing against speed. Reward and
  ; success have to agree about what counts, or a policy is being paid to do the
  ; wrong thing -- so the mask is written out here rather than inherited, and
  ; the two 0.30s are checked against each other by the port's test.
  ;
  ; Note the progress keeps moving after the verdict latches. `once` latches
  ; SUCCESS ONLY.
  (:reward
    (progress (broken-fraction film)
              (:gate 0.95)
              (:gated-by (<= (peak (peel-excess film)) 0.30))))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing -- the quantities nobody has calibrated.
  ;
  ; `peel-front` is the one worth reading beside the broken fraction: a peel is
  ; a FRONT travelling from the tab down the face, so a sheet torn out of the
  ; middle scores a high broken fraction and a front of zero, and those are
  ; different outcomes. `held` separates a failed grasp from a failed peel,
  ; which a run reporting neither leaves indistinguishable -- and since the
  ; re-staging it reads the routine's latch as well as a pinch, so it is true
  ; through the peel rather than only while the pads happen to be on the tab.
  (:observe
    (peel-front film)
    (peel-tension film)
    (rest-tension film)
    (worst-bond film)
    (film-clear film)
    (held film)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; A ROUTINE AND NO GRAPH, since the re-staging. This document shipped the
  ; tape suite's graph `gt` on the argument that nothing about peeling is
  ; tape-specific -- a film laid flat on a block was peeled by the same
  ; straight haul from a latched tab. On the phone that argument stops
  ; holding: the film lies on a face leaning back 30 degrees and comes off
  ; folded back on itself DOWN the face, an arc the graph's one `lift`
  ; landmark cannot state, and a second hand has to hold the phone first.
  ; `handle` keeps the graph; the two film variants ship
  ; routine_peel.py:RoutineFilmPeel, which measures the clip's
  ; choreography in gated legs and reads the film's own published states
  ; between them.
  ;
  ; The tools stay: `sim.get_tab` and `sim.release_anchor` are the layout's
  ; oracle and its anchor, and a future graph over this layout would call
  ; them exactly as the handle graph does.
  (:artifacts
    (:tools   "tools:register_tape_peel_tools")
    (:routine "routine:RoutineFilmPeel")
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Inputs to the ARTIFACT, not statements about the world: what the routine
  ; reads, and nothing it does not. Which arm peels comes from the pairing
  ; above; the pinch is what a rigid pull-tab gets.
  ;
  ; `peel-speed` is how fast the latched tab travels [m/s] -- the episode's
  ; clock, not a load knob, because the cohesive-zone model is rate-independent.
  ; `peel-rise` is the arc's slope in the face's frame: metres the tab rises
  ; off the glass per metre it travels back down it. The pads stay parallel to
  ; the glass so the film folds back on itself; the rise keeps the folded sheet
  ; clear of the hand holding the phone. Both are routine_peel.py's measured
  ; defaults, restated here so a reader asking "how fast, how steep" does not
  ; have to open a policy module -- and `peel-rise` is the knob this variant
  ; exists to have: the peel ANGLE is what the tension budget separates on,
  ; and a document that could not ask for a different one could not produce
  ; the violent peel it is written to catch.
  (:policy-params
    (peel-speed 0.05)
    (peel-rise 0.55)
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier designed)
    (:source "AutoPeel (ICRA 2025) for the adhesion model; sample_videos/film peeling.mp4 for the layout")
    ; A semicolon starts a comment ANYWHERE, including inside what looks like a
    ; quoted string -- BDDL has no strings. The calibration block above is a
    ; comment for exactly that reason: it contains two prose semicolons, and
    ; either of them inside a (:note "...") would have swallowed the closing
    ; paren and the rest of the file with it.
    (:note "No published benchmark defines success for a deformable task like
            this, so the goal is designed. The layout is the clip's -- a phone
            leaning in a stand on the centreline -- placed where both grasps
            and the whole peel arc solve on this robot, which is a little
            further from the arms than the clip has it. What changes from the
            handle variant is the substrate, the film, the second hand and the
            scoring.")
  )

  ; THE ARENA YAM, as a second way to stand this task. `yam_bimanual` is the
  ; same I2RT arm as the native pairing -- 264 mm upper arm, 245 mm forearm,
  ; the same joint ranges -- wearing the arena's `yam_linear_arena` hand
  ; instead of the `linear_4310` blades, and standing 22 mm higher above the
  ; work surface because that robot's mount clears its own play table.
  ;
  ; What that changes, and why this row claims only that it compiles: the two
  ; hands do not share a tool point. The i2rt grasp site sits 134.7 mm down
  ; the gripper body's own axis; the arena's is 130 mm out and **44 mm
  ; laterally off** the wrist axis, with 12.14 mm of tip reach against 35 mm.
  ; Every constant this suite measured against the blades -- the jaw-gap fit,
  ; the grip commands, the clearances -- is a fact about `linear_4310` and
  ; not about the arm. A row says how to stand the robot here, never that the
  ; pairing works: that is the matrix's to discover, and the policy's to earn.
  ;
  ; Measured 2026-09-06 by scripts/matrix.py: built, stepped 10 control steps
  ; finite, work arm within HOME_ERR of its declared home.
  (:hosts
    (:host (:embodiment yam_bimanual (:work-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
           (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
  )
)
